\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Leak at rest, and the Morris–Lecar voltage}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{A membrane that carries only a leak, with positive constant
conductance #{g_{\mathrm{L}}}, obeys
##{C\dot V=I_{\mathrm{app}}-g_{\mathrm{L}}(V-E_{\mathrm{L}}).}
At #{I_{\mathrm{app}}=0} the right-hand side vanishes exactly when
#{V=E_{\mathrm{L}}}. The rest voltage is the [reversal potential](fnip-0066)
of the leak.}

\p{The voltage equation of \ref{fnip-0005} is the same balance with
three ionic currents,
##{C_{\mathrm{M}}\dot V
   = I_{\mathrm{app}}
   - g_{\mathrm{L}}(V-E_{\mathrm{L}})
   - g_{\mathrm{K}} n (V-E_{\mathrm{K}})
   - g_{\mathrm{Ca}} m_{\infty}(V)(V-E_{\mathrm{Ca}}).}
The leak, the potassium current, and the calcium current meet only
in this equation. The equation for #{\dot n} in \ref{fnip-0005}
changes #{n}, and with it the potassium conductance, and adds no
further current.
\citet{(3.1), §3.2, pp. 49–50}{ermentrout2010mathematical}
is that voltage equation.}
